1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,948
Sweetheart, come sit with me a minute.

2
00:00:03,128 --> 00:00:05,629
I drove past the new houses by

3
00:00:05,629 --> 00:00:07,546
the highway last night,

4
00:00:07,546 --> 00:00:08,713
and I counted.

5
00:00:08,893 --> 00:00:10,959
Eleven inflatable snowmen.

6
00:00:11,139 --> 00:00:13,396
Four of them were lying face down

7
00:00:13,396 --> 00:00:14,216
in the yard.

8
00:00:14,396 --> 00:00:15,391
And I thought,

9
00:00:15,391 --> 00:00:18,091
my mother would have laughed until she

10
00:00:18,091 --> 00:00:18,518
cried.

11
00:00:18,698 --> 00:00:21,192
Because in eighty years of Christmases,

12
00:00:21,192 --> 00:00:23,622
I've learned that a beautiful house at

13
00:00:23,622 --> 00:00:25,924
Christmas has nothing to do with how

14
00:00:25,924 --> 00:00:26,755
much you buy.

15
00:00:27,104 --> 00:00:29,281
So tonight I'm going to tell you

16
00:00:29,281 --> 00:00:32,001
the nine decorating mistakes I see every

17
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:32,817
single year.

18
00:00:32,997 --> 00:00:35,562
The ones my mother and Grandma Pearl

19
00:00:35,562 --> 00:00:37,130
would never have made.

20
00:00:37,310 --> 00:00:38,830
And for every one,

21
00:00:38,830 --> 00:00:41,533
I'll tell you exactly what to do

22
00:00:41,533 --> 00:00:42,209
instead.

23
00:00:42,389 --> 00:00:44,665
Most of it costs nothing.

24
00:00:44,845 --> 00:00:46,656
Some of it will save you money.

25
00:00:46,836 --> 00:00:48,533
And all of it will make your

26
00:00:48,533 --> 00:00:51,260
house feel like Christmas the moment somebody

27
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:52,351
walks in the door.

28
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:54,523
Mistake number one.

29
00:00:54,703 --> 00:00:56,085
Too many colors.

30
00:00:56,265 --> 00:00:59,020
Pink and purple and teal and silver

31
00:00:59,020 --> 00:01:00,359
and gold and red,

32
00:01:00,359 --> 00:01:02,012
all on the same tree.

33
00:01:02,192 --> 00:01:05,259
It looks like a candy store exploded,

34
00:01:05,259 --> 00:01:05,756
honey.

35
00:01:05,936 --> 00:01:08,558
Your eye doesn't know where to rest,

36
00:01:08,558 --> 00:01:10,598
so it doesn't rest anywhere,

37
00:01:10,598 --> 00:01:13,221
and the tree just looks busy instead

38
00:01:13,221 --> 00:01:14,168
of beautiful.

39
00:01:14,518 --> 00:01:15,887
Do this instead.

40
00:01:16,067 --> 00:01:18,657
Pick two colors and one metal.

41
00:01:18,837 --> 00:01:20,868
Red and green with gold.

42
00:01:21,048 --> 00:01:23,405
Blue and white with silver.

43
00:01:23,585 --> 00:01:25,710
Cream and red with brass.

44
00:01:25,890 --> 00:01:26,737
That's it.

45
00:01:26,917 --> 00:01:29,250
My mother did red and gold every

46
00:01:29,250 --> 00:01:30,781
year of my childhood,

47
00:01:30,781 --> 00:01:33,551
with the glass balls from Woolworth's,

48
00:01:33,551 --> 00:01:36,248
and people stopped on the sidewalk to

49
00:01:36,248 --> 00:01:37,633
look in our window.

50
00:01:37,813 --> 00:01:39,662
When everything matches,

51
00:01:39,662 --> 00:01:42,434
even cheap ornaments look expensive.

52
00:01:42,783 --> 00:01:45,457
And one more thing about color,

53
00:01:45,457 --> 00:01:46,406
sweetheart.

54
00:01:46,586 --> 00:01:48,785
Pick your colors before you go to

55
00:01:48,785 --> 00:01:50,784
the store, and write them on a

56
00:01:50,784 --> 00:01:52,716
piece of paper in your purse.

57
00:01:52,896 --> 00:01:55,271
Because the store is designed to make

58
00:01:55,271 --> 00:01:57,389
you fall in love with everything.

59
00:01:57,569 --> 00:01:59,579
My mother used to say,

60
00:01:59,579 --> 00:02:01,771
if it isn't red or gold,

61
00:02:01,771 --> 00:02:03,781
it stays on the shelf,

62
00:02:03,781 --> 00:02:04,512
Dorothy.

63
00:02:04,692 --> 00:02:06,478
She said it out loud,

64
00:02:06,478 --> 00:02:09,626
to herself, right there in the aisle.

65
00:02:09,976 --> 00:02:11,554
Mistake number two.

66
00:02:11,734 --> 00:02:14,217
Wrapping the lights around the outside of

67
00:02:14,217 --> 00:02:15,670
the tree like a bandage.

68
00:02:15,850 --> 00:02:17,387
Round and round,

69
00:02:17,387 --> 00:02:19,020
only on the tips.

70
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:21,575
From across the room the tree looks

71
00:02:21,575 --> 00:02:22,864
dark in the middle,

72
00:02:22,864 --> 00:02:23,678
like a hole,

73
00:02:23,678 --> 00:02:25,646
and all the light sits on the

74
00:02:25,646 --> 00:02:26,189
surface.

75
00:02:26,539 --> 00:02:28,164
Do this instead.

76
00:02:28,344 --> 00:02:29,647
Start at the trunk,

77
00:02:29,647 --> 00:02:30,608
at the bottom.

78
00:02:30,788 --> 00:02:33,235
Take each string in toward the trunk

79
00:02:33,235 --> 00:02:35,002
and back out to the tip of

80
00:02:35,002 --> 00:02:36,566
the branch, in and out,

81
00:02:36,566 --> 00:02:37,721
branch by branch,

82
00:02:37,721 --> 00:02:38,741
all the way up.

83
00:02:38,921 --> 00:02:40,536
It takes longer,

84
00:02:40,536 --> 00:02:41,243
I know.

85
00:02:41,423 --> 00:02:44,194
But the tree glows from the inside,

86
00:02:44,194 --> 00:02:45,382
like a lantern.

87
00:02:45,562 --> 00:02:47,898
My father did it every year with

88
00:02:47,898 --> 00:02:50,381
a cigarette in his mouth and Danny

89
00:02:50,381 --> 00:02:51,622
holding the plug.

90
00:02:51,802 --> 00:02:53,358
And here's the rule,

91
00:02:53,358 --> 00:02:56,781
sweetheart: about a hundred lights for every

92
00:02:56,781 --> 00:02:57,793
foot of tree.

93
00:02:58,142 --> 00:03:00,040
And avoid this too,

94
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:00,639
honey.

95
00:03:00,819 --> 00:03:03,298
Never mix the warm white lights and

96
00:03:03,298 --> 00:03:05,636
the cold blue white lights on the

97
00:03:05,636 --> 00:03:06,345
same tree.

98
00:03:06,525 --> 00:03:09,301
The warm ones look like candlelight,

99
00:03:09,301 --> 00:03:11,922
the cold ones look like a hospital

100
00:03:11,922 --> 00:03:14,466
hallway, and together they fight.

101
00:03:14,646 --> 00:03:16,945
Check the box before you buy.

102
00:03:17,125 --> 00:03:19,699
And test every string on the floor

103
00:03:19,699 --> 00:03:21,743
before it goes on the tree,

104
00:03:21,743 --> 00:03:22,500
not after.

105
00:03:22,680 --> 00:03:24,642
Ask me how I know.

106
00:03:24,993 --> 00:03:26,955
Mistake number three.

107
00:03:27,135 --> 00:03:29,521
Ornaments all hung on the very tips

108
00:03:29,521 --> 00:03:30,611
of the branches,

109
00:03:30,611 --> 00:03:32,043
and all at the front,

110
00:03:32,043 --> 00:03:34,156
where you can see them from the

111
00:03:34,156 --> 00:03:34,565
couch.

112
00:03:34,745 --> 00:03:36,832
Then the back of the tree is

113
00:03:36,832 --> 00:03:39,515
bare, and the ornaments in front are

114
00:03:39,515 --> 00:03:43,391
crowded together, clinking every time somebody walks

115
00:03:43,391 --> 00:03:43,615
by.

116
00:03:43,965 --> 00:03:45,439
Do this instead.

117
00:03:45,619 --> 00:03:47,771
Big plain balls go first,

118
00:03:47,771 --> 00:03:50,182
deep inside, near the trunk.

119
00:03:50,362 --> 00:03:52,697
They fill the holes and bounce the

120
00:03:52,697 --> 00:03:53,589
light around.

121
00:03:53,769 --> 00:03:56,055
Then the medium ones in the middle

122
00:03:56,055 --> 00:03:56,997
of the branch.

123
00:03:57,177 --> 00:03:59,294
And your special ornaments,

124
00:03:59,294 --> 00:04:01,018
the ones with a story,

125
00:04:01,018 --> 00:04:03,370
go last, right out on the tips

126
00:04:03,370 --> 00:04:05,408
where people can see them.

127
00:04:05,588 --> 00:04:08,542
Grandma Pearl always said a tree should

128
00:04:08,542 --> 00:04:10,662
have depth, like a good pie.

129
00:04:10,842 --> 00:04:12,502
Layers, honey.

130
00:04:12,682 --> 00:04:13,704
Layers.

131
00:04:14,054 --> 00:04:15,679
Mistake number four.

132
00:04:15,859 --> 00:04:19,085
Throwing out last year's decorations and buying

133
00:04:19,085 --> 00:04:21,350
a whole new theme every December.

134
00:04:21,530 --> 00:04:23,612
I see people at the store with

135
00:04:23,612 --> 00:04:24,445
three carts.

136
00:04:24,625 --> 00:04:27,046
And in January the old ones go

137
00:04:27,046 --> 00:04:28,015
to the curb.

138
00:04:28,195 --> 00:04:29,818
That's not Christmas,

139
00:04:29,818 --> 00:04:30,668
sweetheart.

140
00:04:30,848 --> 00:04:32,090
That's shopping.

141
00:04:32,439 --> 00:04:33,949
Do this instead.

142
00:04:34,129 --> 00:04:36,307
Buy one good thing a year,

143
00:04:36,307 --> 00:04:37,983
and keep it forever.

144
00:04:38,163 --> 00:04:41,029
My mother bought one glass ornament every

145
00:04:41,029 --> 00:04:43,754
Christmas from the time she was married

146
00:04:43,754 --> 00:04:45,292
in nineteen forty-one.

147
00:04:45,472 --> 00:04:47,235
By the time I had my own

148
00:04:47,235 --> 00:04:50,026
house, there were twenty-five of them,

149
00:04:50,026 --> 00:04:52,009
and every one had a year on

150
00:04:52,009 --> 00:04:53,111
it and a story.

151
00:04:53,291 --> 00:04:55,706
That's what makes a tree yours.

152
00:04:55,886 --> 00:04:58,092
Not a theme from a catalog.

153
00:04:58,272 --> 00:04:59,363
A history.

154
00:04:59,714 --> 00:05:01,606
And use what's free.

155
00:05:01,786 --> 00:05:03,713
Pine cones from the park.

156
00:05:03,893 --> 00:05:06,923
Orange slices dried in a low oven.

157
00:05:07,103 --> 00:05:09,681
Cinnamon sticks tied with string.

158
00:05:09,861 --> 00:05:12,350
Holly from the neighbor's bush,

159
00:05:12,350 --> 00:05:13,715
if you ask first.

160
00:05:13,895 --> 00:05:15,668
In nineteen fifty-three,

161
00:05:15,668 --> 00:05:18,476
our whole mantel was pine branches and

162
00:05:18,476 --> 00:05:20,840
pine cones Danny and I picked up

163
00:05:20,840 --> 00:05:22,169
behind the church,

164
00:05:22,169 --> 00:05:24,681
and it was the prettiest mantel on

165
00:05:24,681 --> 00:05:25,494
Elm Street.

166
00:05:25,844 --> 00:05:27,929
And here's how to keep them,

167
00:05:27,929 --> 00:05:28,897
so they last.

168
00:05:29,077 --> 00:05:31,102
When you take the tree down,

169
00:05:31,102 --> 00:05:33,632
wrap each glass ornament in a sheet

170
00:05:33,632 --> 00:05:35,295
of newspaper or tissue,

171
00:05:35,295 --> 00:05:37,320
and put them in egg cartons.

172
00:05:37,500 --> 00:05:39,114
A dozen to a carton.

173
00:05:39,294 --> 00:05:42,106
My mother's egg cartons from the nineteen

174
00:05:42,106 --> 00:05:44,576
sixties are still in my hall closet,

175
00:05:44,576 --> 00:05:46,771
with her handwriting on the lid.

176
00:05:46,951 --> 00:05:48,297
Kitchen balls.

177
00:05:48,477 --> 00:05:50,010
Danny's star.

178
00:05:50,190 --> 00:05:51,548
Pearl's bells.

179
00:05:51,728 --> 00:05:54,192
Never pack them in a plastic bag

180
00:05:54,192 --> 00:05:57,272
all together, or you'll find glitter and

181
00:05:57,272 --> 00:05:59,043
broken glass next year.

182
00:05:59,393 --> 00:06:01,262
Mistake number five.

183
00:06:01,442 --> 00:06:04,019
A house that looks like Christmas but

184
00:06:04,019 --> 00:06:05,412
smells like nothing.

185
00:06:05,592 --> 00:06:08,113
Or worse, smells like one of those

186
00:06:08,113 --> 00:06:11,154
plug-in things that gives you a headache.

187
00:06:11,334 --> 00:06:13,808
You can have the most beautiful tree

188
00:06:13,808 --> 00:06:14,771
in the county,

189
00:06:14,771 --> 00:06:17,177
but if the house doesn't smell like

190
00:06:17,177 --> 00:06:20,064
Christmas, it doesn't feel like Christmas.

191
00:06:20,415 --> 00:06:22,040
Do this instead.

192
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:23,874
Make a simmer pot,

193
00:06:23,874 --> 00:06:24,426
honey.

194
00:06:24,606 --> 00:06:26,583
A pot of water on the back

195
00:06:26,583 --> 00:06:28,941
of the stove with orange peels,

196
00:06:28,941 --> 00:06:30,615
a few cinnamon sticks,

197
00:06:30,615 --> 00:06:32,592
a handful of whole cloves,

198
00:06:32,592 --> 00:06:34,950
and a little splash of vanilla.

199
00:06:35,130 --> 00:06:37,599
Keep it on low all afternoon,

200
00:06:37,599 --> 00:06:40,239
and add water when it gets low.

201
00:06:40,419 --> 00:06:43,215
My mother kept one going every Saturday

202
00:06:43,215 --> 00:06:44,076
in December.

203
00:06:44,256 --> 00:06:46,649
When my father came home from the

204
00:06:46,649 --> 00:06:49,115
plant, he'd open the door and just

205
00:06:49,115 --> 00:06:50,711
stand there breathing.

206
00:06:51,060 --> 00:06:52,459
One more smell,

207
00:06:52,459 --> 00:06:54,416
honey, and it's free.

208
00:06:54,596 --> 00:06:56,512
Bring in a few fresh pine or

209
00:06:56,512 --> 00:06:58,839
cedar branches and lay them on the

210
00:06:58,839 --> 00:07:00,824
mantel or across the top of a

211
00:07:00,824 --> 00:07:01,782
picture frame.

212
00:07:01,962 --> 00:07:03,729
When the heat comes on,

213
00:07:03,729 --> 00:07:06,572
the whole room smells like the woods.

214
00:07:06,752 --> 00:07:08,725
Ask at the tree lot for the

215
00:07:08,725 --> 00:07:11,210
branches they cut off the bottoms.

216
00:07:11,390 --> 00:07:13,809
They'll give them to you for nothing,

217
00:07:13,809 --> 00:07:16,359
because otherwise they throw them away.

218
00:07:16,708 --> 00:07:18,752
Mistake number six.

219
00:07:18,932 --> 00:07:21,399
The yard that looks like a parking

220
00:07:21,399 --> 00:07:22,415
lot for Santa.

221
00:07:22,595 --> 00:07:25,664
Blow-up snowmen, a plastic reindeer,

222
00:07:25,664 --> 00:07:27,539
a light-up candy cane,

223
00:07:27,539 --> 00:07:29,671
a train, a flashing sign,

224
00:07:29,671 --> 00:07:32,314
all crowded on one little lawn,

225
00:07:32,314 --> 00:07:34,701
every one a different color,

226
00:07:34,701 --> 00:07:37,258
blinking at a different speed.

227
00:07:37,608 --> 00:07:39,199
Do this instead.

228
00:07:39,379 --> 00:07:41,713
Pick one thing and do it well.

229
00:07:41,893 --> 00:07:43,934
A wreath on the door with a

230
00:07:43,934 --> 00:07:44,842
big red bow.

231
00:07:45,022 --> 00:07:47,555
White candles in every window,

232
00:07:47,555 --> 00:07:48,568
one in each,

233
00:07:48,568 --> 00:07:51,523
so the house glows from the street.

234
00:07:51,703 --> 00:07:54,358
Maybe a string of white lights along

235
00:07:54,358 --> 00:07:55,465
the porch rail.

236
00:07:55,645 --> 00:07:56,655
That's it.

237
00:07:56,835 --> 00:07:58,890
On Elm Street in the fifties,

238
00:07:58,890 --> 00:08:01,087
every house had a candle in the

239
00:08:01,087 --> 00:08:03,851
window, and walking home from church on

240
00:08:03,851 --> 00:08:06,899
Christmas Eve, the whole street looked like

241
00:08:06,899 --> 00:08:07,679
a painting.

242
00:08:08,028 --> 00:08:09,816
Mistake number seven.

243
00:08:09,996 --> 00:08:12,288
Putting it all up the day after

244
00:08:12,288 --> 00:08:13,027
Halloween.

245
00:08:13,207 --> 00:08:15,519
By the middle of December the tree

246
00:08:15,519 --> 00:08:17,696
is dry, the needles are falling,

247
00:08:17,696 --> 00:08:19,736
you're tired of looking at it,

248
00:08:19,736 --> 00:08:22,320
and Christmas hasn't even started yet.

249
00:08:22,500 --> 00:08:25,127
And a dry tree is dangerous too,

250
00:08:25,127 --> 00:08:26,030
sweetheart.

251
00:08:26,210 --> 00:08:28,009
Never forget that.

252
00:08:28,360 --> 00:08:29,962
Do this instead.

253
00:08:30,142 --> 00:08:31,826
Decorate in steps,

254
00:08:31,826 --> 00:08:33,230
the way we did.

255
00:08:33,410 --> 00:08:36,249
The wreath and the window candles after

256
00:08:36,249 --> 00:08:37,195
Thanksgiving.

257
00:08:37,375 --> 00:08:40,184
The mantel the first week of December.

258
00:08:40,364 --> 00:08:43,046
The tree two weeks before Christmas.

259
00:08:43,226 --> 00:08:44,836
And the manger last,

260
00:08:44,836 --> 00:08:46,205
on Christmas Eve,

261
00:08:46,205 --> 00:08:48,539
with the baby Jesus put in at

262
00:08:48,539 --> 00:08:49,263
midnight.

263
00:08:49,443 --> 00:08:51,729
Every week the house gets a little

264
00:08:51,729 --> 00:08:54,351
more Christmas, and the waiting is half

265
00:08:54,351 --> 00:08:54,889
the joy.

266
00:08:55,069 --> 00:08:57,435
My mother used to say December is

267
00:08:57,435 --> 00:08:58,296
the present.

268
00:08:58,476 --> 00:09:00,728
Christmas is just the bow.

269
00:09:01,079 --> 00:09:02,502
And whatever you do,

270
00:09:02,502 --> 00:09:05,421
sweetheart, water the tree every morning,

271
00:09:05,421 --> 00:09:07,842
keep it three feet from the heater

272
00:09:07,842 --> 00:09:09,123
and the fireplace,

273
00:09:09,123 --> 00:09:11,402
and turn the lights off when you

274
00:09:11,402 --> 00:09:13,466
go to bed or leave the house.

275
00:09:13,646 --> 00:09:15,291
In nineteen fifty-six,

276
00:09:15,291 --> 00:09:17,460
a family on the other side of

277
00:09:17,460 --> 00:09:20,899
Mansfield lost their house on the twenty-ninth

278
00:09:20,899 --> 00:09:22,992
of December from a dry tree.

279
00:09:23,172 --> 00:09:25,674
My father made us check the water

280
00:09:25,674 --> 00:09:27,569
every morning after that,

281
00:09:27,569 --> 00:09:28,478
and so do I.

282
00:09:28,829 --> 00:09:30,384
Mistake number eight.

283
00:09:30,564 --> 00:09:32,848
A house that looks like a magazine

284
00:09:32,848 --> 00:09:35,131
page, but nothing in it belongs to

285
00:09:35,131 --> 00:09:35,870
the family.

286
00:09:36,050 --> 00:09:38,563
All store-bought, all perfect,

287
00:09:38,563 --> 00:09:41,076
all the same as the house next

288
00:09:41,076 --> 00:09:41,495
door.

289
00:09:41,675 --> 00:09:43,957
And the children's paper angels,

290
00:09:43,957 --> 00:09:46,524
the clay ornament from second grade,

291
00:09:46,524 --> 00:09:48,449
go in a box in the basement

292
00:09:48,449 --> 00:09:50,232
because they don't match.

293
00:09:50,582 --> 00:09:52,126
Do this instead.

294
00:09:52,306 --> 00:09:55,347
Give the handmade things the best spot.

295
00:09:55,527 --> 00:09:56,946
Right in front,

296
00:09:56,946 --> 00:09:58,175
at eye level.

297
00:09:58,355 --> 00:10:00,951
I still hang the cardboard star Danny

298
00:10:00,951 --> 00:10:02,846
made in nineteen fifty-one,

299
00:10:02,846 --> 00:10:05,232
with too much glitter and his name

300
00:10:05,232 --> 00:10:06,215
spelled wrong.

301
00:10:06,395 --> 00:10:08,848
Every year somebody asks about it,

302
00:10:08,848 --> 00:10:10,868
and every year I get to tell

303
00:10:10,868 --> 00:10:12,455
them about my brother.

304
00:10:12,635 --> 00:10:15,196
That's what decorations are for,

305
00:10:15,196 --> 00:10:15,677
honey.

306
00:10:15,857 --> 00:10:17,761
They're for telling stories.

307
00:10:18,111 --> 00:10:20,491
Here's a tip for the little ones.

308
00:10:20,671 --> 00:10:23,279
Give every child one ornament of their

309
00:10:23,279 --> 00:10:24,240
own each year,

310
00:10:24,240 --> 00:10:26,369
with their name and the year on

311
00:10:26,369 --> 00:10:26,986
the back.

312
00:10:27,166 --> 00:10:29,236
When they grow up and have their

313
00:10:29,236 --> 00:10:31,241
first tree, you put all of them

314
00:10:31,241 --> 00:10:32,729
in a box and give it to

315
00:10:32,729 --> 00:10:33,053
them.

316
00:10:33,233 --> 00:10:35,844
My daughter cried when I gave her

317
00:10:35,844 --> 00:10:36,240
hers.

318
00:10:36,420 --> 00:10:39,392
Twenty-two ornaments, from the paper one when

319
00:10:39,392 --> 00:10:41,374
she was three to the glass one

320
00:10:41,374 --> 00:10:43,026
the year she got married.

321
00:10:43,206 --> 00:10:44,782
Her whole childhood,

322
00:10:44,782 --> 00:10:45,806
in a shoebox.

323
00:10:46,157 --> 00:10:47,735
Mistake number nine.

324
00:10:47,915 --> 00:10:50,010
Spending all the money on the tree

325
00:10:50,010 --> 00:10:50,811
and the yard,

326
00:10:50,811 --> 00:10:53,153
and then putting Christmas dinner on a

327
00:10:53,153 --> 00:10:54,940
bare table with paper plates.

328
00:10:55,120 --> 00:10:57,911
The table is where the family actually

329
00:10:57,911 --> 00:10:59,822
sits together, sweetheart.

330
00:11:00,002 --> 00:11:02,649
It deserves more than the yard does.

331
00:11:02,999 --> 00:11:04,566
Do this instead.

332
00:11:04,746 --> 00:11:06,638
A white tablecloth,

333
00:11:06,638 --> 00:11:07,335
ironed.

334
00:11:07,515 --> 00:11:10,039
A long runner of pine branches down

335
00:11:10,039 --> 00:11:12,418
the middle, with a few red apples

336
00:11:12,418 --> 00:11:14,148
and three white candles,

337
00:11:14,148 --> 00:11:15,735
all different heights.

338
00:11:15,915 --> 00:11:17,267
And at every plate,

339
00:11:17,267 --> 00:11:19,830
a little name card that the children

340
00:11:19,830 --> 00:11:20,257
write.

341
00:11:20,437 --> 00:11:21,691
That's all.

342
00:11:21,871 --> 00:11:24,582
My mother did that table every year,

343
00:11:24,582 --> 00:11:26,540
and when I close my eyes I

344
00:11:26,540 --> 00:11:29,553
can still see the candlelight on Grandma

345
00:11:29,553 --> 00:11:30,532
Pearl's face.

346
00:11:30,881 --> 00:11:33,071
And avoid this at the table,

347
00:11:33,071 --> 00:11:33,540
honey.

348
00:11:33,720 --> 00:11:36,268
Don't make the centerpiece so tall that

349
00:11:36,268 --> 00:11:38,097
people can't see each other.

350
00:11:38,277 --> 00:11:40,265
Keep it lower than your chin when

351
00:11:40,265 --> 00:11:41,470
you're sitting down.

352
00:11:41,650 --> 00:11:44,645
And never use scented candles at dinner.

353
00:11:44,825 --> 00:11:47,266
They fight with the turkey and the

354
00:11:47,266 --> 00:11:47,554
pie.

355
00:11:47,734 --> 00:11:50,073
Plain white candles at the table,

356
00:11:50,073 --> 00:11:52,412
and save the smell for the stove.

357
00:11:52,762 --> 00:11:55,005
So here's the whole lesson,

358
00:11:55,005 --> 00:11:57,581
if you only remember one thing.

359
00:11:57,761 --> 00:11:59,560
Less, but better.

360
00:11:59,740 --> 00:12:00,890
Two colors.

361
00:12:01,070 --> 00:12:02,939
Lights from the inside.

362
00:12:03,119 --> 00:12:04,942
One good ornament a year.

363
00:12:05,122 --> 00:12:06,573
A pot on the stove.

364
00:12:06,753 --> 00:12:09,005
One beautiful thing in the yard.

365
00:12:09,185 --> 00:12:11,368
Decorate a little at a time.

366
00:12:11,548 --> 00:12:13,707
Put the children's things in front.

367
00:12:13,887 --> 00:12:16,395
And give the table your best.

368
00:12:16,744 --> 00:12:19,145
And tell me in the comments,

369
00:12:19,145 --> 00:12:20,088
sweetheart.

370
00:12:20,268 --> 00:12:22,445
What's the one ornament on your tree

371
00:12:22,445 --> 00:12:23,473
that has a story?

372
00:12:23,653 --> 00:12:25,380
I read every one of them,

373
00:12:25,380 --> 00:12:27,660
and tomorrow I'll tell you how my

374
00:12:27,660 --> 00:12:30,424
mother wrapped presents with no money at

375
00:12:30,424 --> 00:12:32,911
all, so pretty that nobody wanted to

376
00:12:32,911 --> 00:12:33,602
open them.

377
00:12:33,782 --> 00:12:35,292
Good night, honey.

378
00:12:35,472 --> 00:12:36,923
Merry Christmas.
